          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Joseph Conrad</strong><br>\nJoseph Conrad was born Józef Teodor Konrad Korzeniowski in Berdychiv, in what is now Ukraine, to Polish parents. His father was a writer and a translator of French and English literature, which influenced Conrad from an early age. After his parents passed away, Conrad moved to France and later to England, where he began a maritime career that profoundly shaped his future writing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conrad's Seafaring Beginnings</strong><br>\nAt the age of 16, Conrad began his seafaring career by joining the French merchant marine. He later transferred to a British ship and learned English, which he eventually wrote in fluently. His twenty years at sea provided material for many of his later works, giving him a unique insight into the human condition and the imperialist influence of Europe on the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Becoming a British Subject</strong><br>\nIn 1886, after becoming a master mariner, Conrad officially became a British subject. This marked a turning point in his life, as he started to leave his maritime career behind to focus on writing. His experience in different colonies and the high seas served as a backdrop for his exploration of morality and human struggle in his works.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
