          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Conrad's Writing Career</strong><br>\nConrad's writing career spanned over two decades, during which he penned many novels, stories, and essays. Despite initial financial difficulties and mediocre reception, he kept writing. His work often reflected his seafaring experiences and offered a critical view of colonialism, which was unique at the time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Themes in Conrad's Work</strong><br>\nConrad's work is known for its deep psychological insight and its often pessimistic view of life. His stories frequently explore complex moral issues and the inherent darkness in humanity. He is credited with bringing a new narrative style to literature, with novels such as \"Heart of Darkness\" examining the stark realities of colonialism and human greed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Heart of Darkness</strong><br>\nHeart of Darkness, one of Conrad's most famous works, is a novella based on his experience in the Congo. It tells the story of a steamboat captain's journey up the Congo River and his encounter with the brutalities of colonial exploitation. The novella later inspired Francis Ford Coppola's film \"Apocalypse Now,\" which adapted the story to the Vietnam War era.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
